<BaseLayout title="IP geolocation data: limits and safe use — AnalyseSpider" description="Understand IP allocation, routing, and geolocation estimates without treating an address as identity or precise physical location." canonicalPath="/guides/ip-geolocation-data" pageType="article" publishedTime="2026-08-22" modifiedTime="2026-08-28" breadcrumbs={[{name:"Home",path:"/"},{name:"Guides",path:"/guides"},{name:"IP geolocation data",path:"/guides/ip-geolocation-data"}]}>
  <PageLead title="IP geography is an estimate, not identity." intro="Read allocation, routing, and commercial location data as dated signals with failure modes—not as proof of a person or precise place." />
  <section class="content-grid">
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Three different questions</h2>
      <h3>Who received the address block?</h3>
      <p>IANA coordinates the global address pools and allocates large blocks to the five Regional Internet Registries. RIRs and local registries then allocate or assign resources to network operators. A registration record therefore describes number-resource administration. It is not a record of the person currently using one address.</p>
      <h3>Who announces the route?</h3>
      <p>An Autonomous System Number identifies a routing domain used by network operators. A route-origin observation can associate a prefix with an AS at a particular time. It can change, and anycast can announce the same prefix from several places. That makes ASN and route data useful for network diagnosis, not proof of a user's country or city.</p>
      <h3>Where does a dataset place it?</h3>
      <p>Commercial and public geolocation datasets estimate a location using their own signals and update schedules. RIPE NCC explicitly says it is not a geolocation provider; its database may contain user-maintained location attributes, while RIPEstat labels third-party geolocation as such. Agreement between products does not turn an estimate into precise physical evidence.</p>

      <h2>Four common reasons a location estimate moves</h2>
      <dl class="facts-list"><div><dt>Mobile networks</dt><dd>Traffic can exit through a carrier gateway far from the device.</dd></div><div><dt>VPN or proxy</dt><dd>The visible address belongs to the exit service, not the user's access network.</dd></div><div><dt>Anycast</dt><dd>One prefix can be announced from multiple sites and routed to a nearby edge.</dd></div><div><dt>Reassignment</dt><dd>Allocation, routing and commercial datasets can update on different schedules.</dd></div></dl>

      <h2>Safe product patterns</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Use country-level estimates to preselect a reversible option, not to silently lock it.</li>
        <li>Show a user-visible override and retain the user's explicit preference.</li>
        <li>Record dataset name, edition or observation time, and expected accuracy level.</li>
        <li>Separate network ownership, route origin, and estimated geography in the data model.</li>
        <li>Do not use an IP estimate alone for identity, sanctions, fraud, eligibility, or access decisions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why the former database is not here</h2>
      <p>The historical domain advertised IP-country data and related downloads. No former database, update contract, license, or distribution right transferred. This page is an independently written limitations guide and does not offer those files.</p>

      <h2>Primary sources</h2>
      <p>Sources checked 28 August 2026.</p>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.iana.org/numbers">IANA Number Resources</a> — address and ASN coordination and the RIR allocation chain.</li><li><a href="https://www.iana.org/assignments/as-numbers">IANA Autonomous System Number registry</a> — current ASN purpose and allocation records.</li><li><a href="https://docs.db.ripe.net/Related-Software-and-Tools/Geolocation-in-the-RIPE-Database/">RIPE Database geolocation documentation</a> — RIPE NCC's explicit provider boundary.</li><li><a href="https://stat.ripe.net/docs/getting-started/ripestat-data-sources">RIPEstat data sources</a> — separation of registry, routing and third-party geolocation sources.</li></ul>
    </article>
    <aside class="side-note">
      <h3>Minimum metadata</h3>
      <dl class="facts-list">
        <div><dt>Source</dt><dd>Named dataset or observation</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Edition</dt><dd>Version or date</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Granularity</dt><dd>Country / region / city</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Confidence</dt><dd>Defined, not decorative</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Override</dt><dd>User-controlled fallback</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </aside>
  </section>
